Day 9 – Enable Termination Protection on an EC2 Instance (AWS)

Objective

During the migration, the Nautilus DevOps team created an EC2 instance without termination protection. Enable termination protection on nautilus-ec2 in us-east-1.

Configuration
Setting                 Value
Instance Name           nautilus-ec2
Instance ID             i-027a346c4148ed26f
Instance Type           t2.micro
Availability Zone       us-east-1a
Termination Protection  Enabled

Steps Performed
1. Opened the EC2 Console and filtered instances by Instance state = running.
2. Selected nautilus-ec2.
3. Opened Actions -> Instance settings -> Change termination (deletion) protection.
4. Ticked Enable and clicked Save.
5. Confirmed the banner "Successfully enabled termination protection for instance i-027a346c4148ed26f. The instance can't be deleted."

Important Concepts Learned
- Termination protection (DisableApiTermination): Blocks termination of the instance through the console, CLI and API until the setting is turned off.
- It does not block everything: OS-initiated shutdowns with shutdown behavior set to terminate, Auto Scaling terminations and Spot interruptions still terminate the instance.
- Stop protection is separate. It prevents stopping, not deleting. Both can be enabled together.
- Not a security boundary: Anyone with ec2:ModifyInstanceAttribute permission can turn it off, so IAM policies and CloudTrail monitoring are still needed.

Key Takeaway
Termination protection guards against accidental deletion of important instances. Enable it on any instance that holds non-replaceable data or serves production traffic, and check it on instances created outside the standard process.
